L000323	\textit{GME at finite temperature.}---To extend our multipartite entanglement measure to finite temperatures, we employ thermal pure quantum (TPQ) states~\cite{Sugiura:2013pla}, which reproduce thermal expectation values while remaining pure. The canonical TPQ state is defined as
L000324	\begin{align}
L000325		|\Psi(\beta)\rangle \equiv e^{-{\beta\over 2}H}|\psi\rangle ,
L000326	\end{align}
L000327	where $H$ is the system Hamiltonian and $|\psi\rangle$ is a random state, yielding
L000328	\begin{align}
L000329		\frac{\overline{\langle \Psi_\beta | \cO |\Psi_\beta \rangle}}
L000330		{\overline{\langle \Psi_\beta | \Psi_\beta \rangle}}
L000331		= \frac{1}{Z(\beta)} \tr(\cO e^{-\beta H}) .
L000332	\end{align}
L000333	We generalize this construction to multipartite systems by defining the multipartite thermal pure quantum (MTPQ) state
L000334	\begin{align}
L000335		|\Psi_\alpha\rangle \equiv \bigotimes_{i=1}^n e^{-{\alpha\over 2}H^{(k)}} |\psi\rangle ,
L000336	\end{align}
L000337	which we interpret as a thermal 2-uniform state, characterized by factorized two-party reduced density matrices
L000338	\begin{align}
L000339		\rho_{A_{j_1}A_{j_k}} = \rho_{\text{\tiny th}}(\beta_{j_1}) \otimes \rho_{\text{\tiny th}}(\beta_{j_k}) .
L000340	\end{align}
L000341	The corresponding bipartite L-entropy satisfies
L000342	\begin{align}\label{Ltherm2uni}
L000343		\ell_{A_iA_j} = 2\min\!\big(S_{\text{\tiny th}}(\beta_i),S_{\text{\tiny th}}(\beta_j)\big) .
L000344	\end{align}
